WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.033
-How are you doing?
How is everything going?

00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:03.367
-Life is very, very good.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:05.000
-Hey, you look great.
-Thank you.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.901
I'm glad to be working
in theater in New York.

00:00:07.901 --> 00:00:11.834
-Yeah. I think you Zoomed
with us last time,

00:00:11.834 --> 00:00:12.968
which was very nice of you
to do.

00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:14.601
-Yes.
-Thank you for helping us.

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:16.234
-That was a long time ago now.
-Yeah, well.

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:18.267
-Well, not as long as it feels.
-Yeah, exactly.

00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:21.000
But we do appreciate it
'cause we -- Thank you.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.567
We needed guests.
-You stayed in business.

00:00:22.567 --> 00:00:23.968
-Yes, we stayed in business,
yeah.

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:25.434
[ Laughter ]

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:30.067
I was asking about your husband
on Instagram because he had

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.267
a thing that he was doing,
and I go,

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:33.400
"How long has he been
doing this?"

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.667
He's a great artist.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:35.701
-He is a great artist.

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:37.367
-And did you always know
he was a painter?

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:41.000
-I knew that he was --
He studied art in school.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.567
When I was met him,
he was a graphic designer.

00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.467
And this is so funny.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.934
We've been together almost
20 years next month it'll be,

00:00:46.934 --> 00:00:50.701
and I --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.701 --> 00:00:53.467
-Very nice.
-You know it's funny.

00:00:53.467 --> 00:00:56.501
You understand getting applause
for a length of a relationship

00:00:56.501 --> 00:00:57.801
the longer it goes on.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:57.801 --> 00:01:00.200
Before it was always like,
it's just a pleasure.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.767
And it is still just a pleasure.

00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:04.100
But it's like, yeah,
it's an accomplishment.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.734
-Yeah, that's right. 20 years.
Not too shabby.

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.901
-We've been together -- for the
first 18 years, I didn't see him

00:01:09.901 --> 00:01:12.300
paint or draw
more than twice I'd say.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.901
And he's kind of had
a renaissance.

00:01:13.901 --> 00:01:15.434
He's back in school
studying design.

00:01:15.434 --> 00:01:17.334
But, yeah, he's painting dogs.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.400
-Yeah, here's a painting.
-Those are ours, yeah.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:20.634
-Here's a painting of you.

00:01:20.634 --> 00:01:21.968
-That's me with
one of these dogs.

00:01:21.968 --> 00:01:23.300
-Are you sleeping here?

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.267
-No, I'm actually watching TV.

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:26.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:28.267
I'm sure I'm watching you,
Jimmy.

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.834
-Oh, wow, thank you.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:29.834 --> 00:01:31.067
-I lay down.

00:01:31.067 --> 00:01:33.033
I'm horizontal
when we watch TV normally

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.334
and the dog sometimes snoozes
on my chest, yeah.

00:01:35.334 --> 00:01:37.734
-Yeah. Your husband -- You said
he's going back at school?

00:01:37.734 --> 00:01:40.033
He's at Parsons?
-He's at Parsons for --

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.634
[ Woman cheers ]
No relation.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.767
[ Laughter ]
For interior design.

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.200
I wish. Always when I came here,
I was like --

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:49.033
"We're not related to anybody?"
"No, nobody."

00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:50.534
-Wow, nothing.
-It's sad.

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:52.067
-Yeah, it is sad.

00:01:52.067 --> 00:01:53.334
You know what I wanted to ask
you about.

00:01:53.334 --> 00:01:55.601
"The Big Bang Theory,"
obviously, everyone loves.

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:58.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:58.133 --> 00:01:59.767
It's just as hot as it ever was.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.567
But everyone has been talking
about it lately

00:02:01.567 --> 00:02:03.934
because of this new oral history
that just came out

00:02:03.934 --> 00:02:06.567
where they interviewed you
for how long?

00:02:06.567 --> 00:02:08.701
-I did like 20 hours of
interviews with it.

00:02:08.701 --> 00:02:11.734
But honestly it was really the
only way I thought it was worth

00:02:11.734 --> 00:02:13.601
doing because I thought this
isn't the kind of thing --

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:16.567
if they're just going to talk
for an hour or two, it's like --

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.901
And so, I was like,
"Cool, let's do it."

00:02:18.901 --> 00:02:22.334
And it was really interesting
because it was sort of

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:25.634
a decompression of those
12 years that I didn't realize

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:28.434
how helpful that would be
because other people would jog

00:02:28.434 --> 00:02:31.100
Memories when she'd come back to
you with other questions.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.167
They're like, "They said."
I'm like, "That's right.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:35.167
That happened" or whatever.
And it was cool.

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:37.701
-You go, "Oh, I know --"

00:02:37.701 --> 00:02:39.200
-Oh, I had my own little box

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.334
of what that time was
in its own way.

00:02:41.334 --> 00:02:42.901
-Well, you lived it.
-Yeah.

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:45.434
I know where all the bodies
are buried, I like to say.

00:02:45.434 --> 00:02:47.767
[ Laughter ]
But yeah.

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:51.033
-But it's pretty -- And do you
see people coming up to you

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:53.701
still that are new fans
or even getting --

00:02:53.701 --> 00:02:56.267
-I don't know about new fans,
but I will tell you that

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.334
since I've been --
we haven't done it

00:02:57.334 --> 00:02:58.801
for three years now or so.

00:02:58.801 --> 00:03:01.667
And jobs that I'm working,
people who I guess didn't watch

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:04.234
it all that I'll be working with
suddenly will be like,

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:05.501
"I watched an episode
last night.

00:03:05.501 --> 00:03:07.734
You were very good on it."
I said, "Thank you."

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:09.534
But they had never seen it
before.

00:03:09.534 --> 00:03:10.667
But I guess they're working with
me like,

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:12.000
"What the hell
did he do on there?"

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.801
[ Laughter ]
It's so funny.

00:03:13.801 --> 00:03:15.033
-Oh, my gosh.
It's the biggest show ever.

00:03:15.033 --> 00:03:17.234
-It's sweet, yeah.
-I also heard you auditioned for

00:03:17.234 --> 00:03:18.634
another big show, "The Office."

00:03:18.634 --> 00:03:20.734
-I did audition for
"The Off--" Yeah.

00:03:20.734 --> 00:03:24.734
I auditioned for the role of
Jim, John Krasinski's role.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:26.734
And I auditioned for Rainn
Wilson's role.

00:03:26.734 --> 00:03:30.033
What was he called? Dwight.

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.534
It's really a great example --

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:33.501
-I could see you doing
either one.

00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:35.267
-I could see me doing
either one.

00:03:35.267 --> 00:03:37.501
I also felt like I sort of fell
in between them.

00:03:37.501 --> 00:03:39.334
And it was a great example of
the kind of thing

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:42.501
where I don't get it and you see
it air and you see who gets it

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.067
and you're like,
"Oh, I would have never --

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:45.467
That's not what I would've done
with it,"

00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:48.033
and what they're doing with it
was so powerful and strong

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:50.634
and unique in both of those
roles that it's like,

00:03:50.634 --> 00:03:52.534
"That's what should've
happened."

00:03:52.534 --> 00:03:54.234
It's the same way -- Like, I
think Rainn would have been

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:58.968
a very good Sheldon, but it
wouldn't have been like me.

00:03:58.968 --> 00:04:01.033
-It would've been different.
-It would've been different.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:03.234
I don't even think --
He didn't audition.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:05.100
He was on "The Office," so.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.267
What do I know? He could have
been moonlighting.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:08.767
[ Laughter ]

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:11.534
-Before we get into your show,
I also want to mention that

00:04:11.534 --> 00:04:13.367
you're in a new film called
"Spoiler Alert."

00:04:13.367 --> 00:04:15.634
-Yes.
-Without spoiling anything --

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:17.667
[ Laughter ]
-It's already spoiled.

00:04:17.667 --> 00:04:21.300
It's a movie based on
the true-life memoir

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.801
of TV journalist
Michael Ausiello.

00:04:23.801 --> 00:04:26.701
He and his partner, who
eventually became his husband,

00:04:26.701 --> 00:04:30.000
lived in New York on a very
similar trajectory of time

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.267
to me and my husband
here in New York.

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:38.200
And his husband battled cancer
and eventually passed away.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.968
So this is their journey
through that.

00:04:40.968 --> 00:04:45.734
And I think one of the most
interesting things about it is

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:48.667
the way that Michael, as a
person and then the character

00:04:48.667 --> 00:04:50.734
in the movie, he dealt with --
his mother passed away

00:04:50.734 --> 00:04:52.801
from cancer when he was
a much younger man.

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:55.834
And it really affected, I think,
the way he tackled his husband's

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:59.534
having cancer, like, really
trying to take action

00:04:59.534 --> 00:05:01.934
in ways that he couldn't have
as a child.

00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:05.400
And, yeah, it was one of
those things that's --

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.601
it's a heartbreaking and really
tumultuous journey

00:05:08.601 --> 00:05:11.901
but also one of those that opens
your eyes to the life you do

00:05:11.901 --> 00:05:15.067
have and the love you can share
in a way that without it

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.567
you don't always see as clearly.
You know what I mean?

00:05:17.567 --> 00:05:19.133
It's like you can't really
live and love

00:05:19.133 --> 00:05:20.968
without experiencing heartbreak.

00:05:20.968 --> 00:05:24.901
And I could cry.
I'm not going to.

00:05:24.901 --> 00:05:27.167
[ Audience awws ]
[ Laughs ]

00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:28.601
Keep it together.

00:05:28.601 --> 00:05:30.400
-It's in select theaters
December 2nd.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.234
It opens nationwide
December 16th.

00:05:32.234 --> 00:05:33.934
It is beautiful.
And check it out.

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.434
Let's talk about
"A Man of No Importance."

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:41.734
Do you love doing off-Broadway
as much as Broadway?

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:44.501
-Yes. This is my second
off-Broadway show.

00:05:44.501 --> 00:05:46.901
I did one when I first moved to
New York in 2001.

00:05:46.901 --> 00:05:50.501
And it's been a long time,
long enough that --

00:05:50.501 --> 00:05:54.501
well, 21 years, that we were
rehearsing in this space.

00:05:54.501 --> 00:05:56.701
It's very small,
200 seats or so.

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:58.868
And we're in and out of it.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:00.634
They're so close that we were

00:06:00.634 --> 00:06:01.868
getting close
to audience coming.

00:06:01.868 --> 00:06:03.834
I started getting very uptight
about it.

00:06:03.834 --> 00:06:08.100
I was like, "Woof, they're going
to be right here."

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:13.300
And then then they arrived
and it's wonderful.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:15.067
For one thing, it's something
I'm very familiar with

00:06:15.067 --> 00:06:16.267
and kind of hadn't
thought about.

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:18.033
That's all the theater I did
growing up.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:19.701
It was always
in weird little places.

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:22.400
It was never nice, grand
Broadway stages or whatever.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.100
-Yeah.
-But, yeah.

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:28.601
You know, the show itself is
a very intimate show about

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:30.868
a small-town group of people
in Dublin.

00:06:30.868 --> 00:06:34.734
And it's partly a man coming to
terms with who he is,

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:37.100
but it's a lot about what it is
to make theater.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.901
And so there's that whole feel
about it.

00:06:39.901 --> 00:06:43.133
I keep saying, if you've never
seen anything in a theater,

00:06:43.133 --> 00:06:44.901
this is the kind of show
that you would go and see

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:46.701
and be completely addicted.

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:48.467
You would be like,
"I want to do that."

00:06:48.467 --> 00:06:51.033
What they're making there,
it's just so --

00:06:51.033 --> 00:06:52.534
it's the essence of creativity.
I just love it.

00:06:52.534 --> 00:06:53.934
-Isn't that great? I love that.
-Yeah.

00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:56.334
-And not only is it awesome,
it's already been extended.

00:06:56.334 --> 00:06:58.300
It hasn't even started yet.
-Yes.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.567
We haven't even opened.
-The demand, yeah.

00:07:00.567 --> 00:07:01.801
-That was a touch of the Dublin.

00:07:01.801 --> 00:07:04.267
-It was. You did a little open.
Yeah, of course.

00:07:04.267 --> 00:07:08.167
Before we go, I heard that this
story that when you were a kid,

00:07:08.167 --> 00:07:10.801
you used to imitate
Johnny Carson.

00:07:10.801 --> 00:07:13.467
-Oh, yes. Coming behind
the drapes, yes.

00:07:13.467 --> 00:07:14.901
I was fixated
on a couple of things.

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:17.300
I also wanted a sweater
in the closet of Mr. Rogers.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.734
Didn't want to host his show.

00:07:18.734 --> 00:07:20.567
But I didn't want to host
"The Tonight Show,"

00:07:20.567 --> 00:07:22.033
but I wanted to come
out of the drapes,

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:24.667
which was probably
a clue for something.

00:07:24.667 --> 00:07:26.234
But anyway.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:26.234 --> 00:07:27.567
That's two different things.

00:07:27.567 --> 00:07:28.801
-Would you like to come
through the curtains?

00:07:28.801 --> 00:07:30.200
-Oh, Jimmy! Really?

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.367
I'll do it.
-Yeah?

00:07:32.367 --> 00:07:34.067
-Yeah.
-Come here.

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:36.300
You'll hit that mark at the end.
Go to that mark.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.234
I'll meet you there.
-You're not getting a monologue.

00:07:38.234 --> 00:07:39.634
That's all I'm saying.
-No, no, no, we're not giving

00:07:39.634 --> 00:07:40.734
you a monologue.
We'll cut to there.

00:07:40.734 --> 00:07:41.801
I'll introduce you.

00:07:41.801 --> 00:07:43.767
And then, can we do that,
like an intro?

00:07:43.767 --> 00:07:45.234
-Yes.
-Alright, and I'll just --

00:07:45.234 --> 00:07:47.234
I'll be the announcer.
[ Drumroll ]

00:07:49.701 --> 00:07:54.934
And here's your host,
Jim Parsons!

00:07:54.934 --> 00:08:00.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:09.767
♪♪

00:08:09.767 --> 00:08:11.901
How did it feel?
Like the drapes?

00:08:11.901 --> 00:08:13.968
-It's really comfortable
actually.

00:08:13.968 --> 00:08:16.634
-It feels good, right?
-I'm probably a real egomaniac

00:08:16.634 --> 00:08:18.167
because I was like, "That feels
very comfortable."

00:08:18.167 --> 00:08:20.200
-Yeah, you're a natural.
Jim Parsons, everyone.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.701
Go check out
"A Man of No Importance."

00:08:23.701 --> 00:08:26.334
It's in previews now
at Classic Stage Company.

00:08:26.334 --> 00:08:28.467
More "Tonight Show"
after the break, everybody.

